From 23739ada3c9ee262769fd872bdca0e6c255f923e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:33:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NSArrayController: equal objects in the content, as macOS has
 them

The controller found objects in its arranged objects with
-indexOfObject:, so of several equal objects only the first was ever
selected, an object inserted or added was selected as the first one
equal to it rather than where it was put, and removing by arranged index
went through -removeObject:, which removed every equal object from the
content.  Two new empty dictionaries are equal, so adding a second one
with Add selected the first, and removing one removed both.

On macOS 15:

- -setSelectedObjects:, -addSelectedObjects: and -removeSelectedObjects:
  act on every arranged object equal to one given, whether or not the
  one given is in the content;
- the selection kept across a rearrangement (sorting, filtering) is
  every object equal to one that was selected;
- an object inserted or added is selected at the index it was put at,
  and only that one;
- -removeObjectAtArrangedObjectIndex:, -removeObjectsAtArrangedObjectIndexes:
  and so -remove: remove the objects at those indexes only, and the
  selection that stays is the selected objects not removed, or the next
  object; -removeObject: and -removeObjects: remove every object equal
  to one given.

So objects to select are looked up by equality over all the arranged
objects (through a set, so by -hash and -isEqual:), inserted ones by
identity, and removal by index removes those objects by identity from
the content, a bound content array and the arranged objects.

Tests/gui/NSArrayController/equalObjects.m checks each of these with
objects of a class equal by name and with empty dictionaries; it passes
on macOS 15, and without the change 15 of its 20 checks fail.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
